course: everyday
lesson: 106
title: Kitchen floors, food safety and the microwave
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Germs grow fast in food left warm, so food goes in the fridge, the fridge door stays shut and mouldy food is thrown away.
explain: A microwave heats food by making the water in it jiggle, but metal makes sparks, sealed things burst and a whole egg explodes.
explain: The kitchen is no place to run: knives, hot pans and slippery floors make a fall there much worse.
explain: For example, a girl in Florida who sees a fuzzy green spot on the bread throws the whole loaf away, because mould spreads threads you cannot see.
fact: Walk in the kitchen and play elsewhere while cooking happens.
fact: Wipe up spills at once or tell someone, because wet or greasy floors make people slip.
fact: Mould spreads threads through soft food like bread further than you can see, so throw the whole loaf away.
fact: Germs grow fast at room temperature, so cooked food left out for hours can be unsafe.
fact: An open fridge door lets food warm up and spoil, and wastes electricity.
fact: Never share food with someone who has an allergy; even a tiny amount can cause a dangerous reaction.
fact: Use only microwave-safe dishes: metal, foil and gold rims make sparks and can start a fire.
fact: Never microwave a whole egg in its shell, because steam builds up and it explodes.
fact: Never run a microwave empty, because nothing absorbs the energy and it can be damaged.
fact: If something catches fire inside a microwave, keep the door shut, switch it off at the wall and call a grown-up.
fact: A fire in a closed microwave runs out of air.
fact: Take the lid off or leave a gap when heating a jar or container, because pressure can make it burst.
exercise: Your little cousin is running around while dinner is being cooked. What should he do? | Walk in the kitchen and play somewhere else | Run, but only near the fridge | Run if he is careful | Play hide and seek under the table while people cook
exercise: Juice is spilt on the kitchen floor. What do you do? | Wipe it up straight away or tell someone | Leave it to dry by itself | Walk around it carefully and then forget all about it | Cover it with a towel and leave it
exercise: There are small fuzzy green spots on the bread. What do you do? | Throw the whole loaf away | Cut off the spots and eat the rest | Toast it to kill the mould | Eat only the slices without spots
exercise: Pizza was left out on the table all night. What is right? | Ask a grown-up, as it may be unsafe | Eat it, because pizza never goes bad | Put it back in the fridge and eat it later | Smell it, and if it smells fine eat it
exercise: You notice the fridge door is not shut. What do you do? | Close it properly | Leave it, it closes by itself | Open it wider to let it air | Unplug the fridge for a rest
exercise: Your brother has a peanut allergy and wants to taste your peanut butter. What do you do? | Do not share, and tell a grown-up if he eats some | Give him just a tiny taste | Let him have some if he says he feels fine at the moment | Give him some with water to wash it down
exercise: You want to warm soup in a metal bowl in the microwave. What is right? | Use a microwave-safe dish, never metal or foil | Metal is fine in a microwave if you only heat it briefly | Cover the metal bowl with foil | Use the bowl with the gold rim instead
exercise: Why do metal and foil not go in the microwave? | They can make sparks and start a fire | They make all the food inside taste of metal for days | They make the microwave too cold | They slow the turntable down
exercise: Your friend wants to cook an egg in its shell in the microwave. What do you say? | Never do that; steam builds up and it explodes | Yes, it cooks quickly that way and the shell keeps it clean | Only if you put it on high power | Yes, but put a lid on top
exercise: Someone has switched on the microwave with nothing inside. What should you do? | Stop it, as running empty can damage it | Leave it, it is fine to run empty for a while | Open the door while it runs | Put your hand in to check
exercise: Something catches fire inside the microwave. What do you do? | Keep the door shut, switch it off at the wall and call a grown-up | Open the door straight away and throw a cup of water in | Open the door to let the smoke out | Take the burning food out quickly
exercise: Why keep the microwave door shut if there is a fire inside? | The fire runs out of air | The door keeps the fire hotter | The fire will go out faster with air | The glass makes the flames smaller
exercise: You want to heat a sealed jar of sauce in the microwave. What is right? | Take the lid off or leave a gap | Shake it well and heat it sealed | Wrap it in foil first | Heat it twice as long sealed
test: Ruby in Melbourne finds blue fuzz on one corner of a loaf. Her friend says, «Just cut it off.» Who is right? | Ruby should throw it away, as the mould spreads unseen | The friend, because mould only ever stays on the surface where you can see it | The friend, if she toasts the bread | Both, as mould is harmless
test: Which is true about food left out in a warm room? | Germs grow fast at room temperature | Food lasts longer outside the fridge | Germs can only grow in the fridge | Cooked food can never go bad
test: Why can carrying a hot pan across a wet floor be very dangerous? | Someone could slip and be burnt as well as hurt | Wet floors make the pans much heavier to hold | Water makes pans hotter | Pans slide off wet hands only
test: A girl in a café gives her friend with a nut allergy a «tiny bit» of a nutty cake. What is true? | Even a tiny amount can cause a dangerous reaction | A tiny bit is always safe | Allergies only matter when you eat a big meal of nuts | Allergies stop after age eight
test: Jack heats leftovers on a plate with a gold rim and sees sparks. What should he have used? | A plain microwave-safe dish | A metal tray instead | The same plate covered in foil | A metal pan with a lid
test: What would most likely happen to an egg in its shell in the microwave? | Steam builds up and it bursts | It cooks perfectly like a boiled egg | It stays raw inside the shell | It turns into an omelette
test: Why does leaving the fridge door open waste electricity? | The fridge must work harder to cool the warm air | The fridge light uses up all the power | Cold air makes the fridge switch off | The open door makes the motor turn backwards and use more power
test: A plastic tub with a tight lid swells in the microwave. What is the problem? | Pressure is building and it can burst | The tub is getting colder inside as the food heats up | The food is turning into air | The microwave is too weak
test: Why should small children not run in the kitchen during cooking? | Knives, hot pans and slippery floors make falls dangerous | Running about makes the food on the hob cook unevenly | Children are not allowed in kitchens | Running uses up the oxygen in the room
test: bit of mould on the bread, i'll just pull that bit off | Throw the whole loaf, mould spreads further than you see | Pull that bit off and eat the rest, it's fine | Toast it to kill the mould | Cut the crust off and eat it
test: can i do a boiled egg in the microwave, shell n all | No, a whole egg in its shell explodes | Yes, two minutes on full power is perfect | Yes, if you cover it with foil | Yes, if you do it on high
test: somethings on fire in the microwave!! open it?? | Keep the door shut, switch off at the wall, call a grown-up | Open it and throw water in | Open it a crack to let the smoke out first | Take the plate out quickly
test: heating my soup in the bowl w the gold rim | No, gold rims make sparks, use a microwave-safe dish | Yes, gold rims are totally safe to heat for a bit | Yes, if you heat it slowly | Yes, if it is only for a minute
explain: A boy in Leeds left a spill of oil on the kitchen floor; his mum slipped carrying a pot and burnt her leg.
explain: A girl in Texas put a metal spoon in a bowl in the microwave; it sparked and caught fire, and the family had to switch off and keep the door shut until it went out.
fact: Metal in a microwave can spark and start a fire.
fact: Undercooked chicken, still pink inside, and unwashed hands after touching raw meat can give people food poisoning.
test: spilt juice on the floor, i'll clean it later | Wipe it now, someone could slip and get hurt | Leave it, it dries | Put a towel on top of it and walk off, it's fine | Only clean it if it's oil
test: microwave sparking, there's a fork in the bowl | Stop it, keep the door shut and get an adult | Open the door quickly and grab the fork out of it | Throw water on it | Let it keep going
test: chicken looks a bit pink inside, eat it anyway? | No, cook it till it's done all through or you can get sick | Yes, pink in the middle just means it's nice and juicy | Yes, if you add ketchup | Only if it's hot outside
test: omg a girl put a spoon in the microwave n it exploded n the whole kitchen burnt down, my cousin said. what's probably exaggerated | The whole kitchen; metal does spark and can start a fire | That metal left in a microwave really can make sparks and flashes | That it was a spoon left in the bowl | Nothing, a spoon always burns down a kitchen
test: did u hear a kid shared his peanut sandwich with Ella n she had to go to hospital, everyone's blaming Ella for being fussy. what's the real lesson | Never share food with someone who has an allergy | Ella should eat whatever she is offered at lunch | Allergies only matter when the food is cooked | Tiny amounts of food never cause a reaction
test: Max's mum slipped on oil he spilt n burnt her leg, everyone's saying it's all his fault n he's grounded forever lol, tell the class? | No, it is private; the lesson is wipe spills up at once | Yes, so everyone knows Max is careless | Yes, but only the part about being grounded | Yes, because it is funny that he is in trouble and everyone should laugh at him
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a whole egg in its shell must not be heated in a microwave oven. | Steam accumulates within the shell and it explodes | The shell absorbs all of the energy of the oven and catches fire | The egg is rendered poisonous by the heating | The yolk is cooked before the white is warmed
exam: Whereas mould is observed upon one slice of a loaf, determine the appropriate course of action. | Discard the whole loaf, as the threads spread unseen | Remove the visible mould with a knife and eat the remainder | Toast the slice so as to destroy the mould | Discard only the slice upon which it is seen
exam: Pursuant to an outbreak of fire within a microwave oven, ascertain the correct procedure. | Keep the door shut, switch off at the wall and call an adult | Open the door at once and throw cold water upon the flames inside | Remove the burning food by hand and run it under a tap | Open the door so that the smoke may leave the oven
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning cooked food left at room temperature. | Germs grow rapidly, so after some hours it may be unsafe | Cooked food remains safe at room temperature for weeks at a time | Germs cannot grow in food that has once been cooked | It becomes safe again once it has cooled completely
